    "body": "EICMA 2026 • 2027 Suzuki SV-7S Unveiled at the EICMA Show in Italy: I’m Suddenly More Nostalgic Than Ever\nNew Gas Turbine Will Keep AI Cool Before It Gets to Power Supersonic Passenger Plane\n19 photos\nPhoto: Boom Supersonic\nI usually take breakthrough news of startups developing revolutionary aircraft with a grain of salt. Not because these guys are not serious about their projects, but because, most of the time, seriousness is not enough to get these projects off the ground, and many of the companies imagining them go bust. But not the guys over at Boom Supersonic, it seems.\nThe company first caught my eye some six years ago, when I learned of the supersonic airplane it was working on, the Overture . Capable of carrying 80 people at a time, it should fly at speeds of Mach 1.3 (997 mph/1,605 kph), which is enough to get them from one American coast to the other in one and a half hours.\nThe design is still in the works, and the last time I heard anything about it was when a jet-powered test bed called XB-1 began testing (the program has ended in the meantime), but chances are the production version is still a long time away.\nUnlike many other aviation startups, especially the ones struggling to stay afloat, Boom diversified its business at the end of last year, when it announced it will make a natural gas turbine with a capacity of 42 megawatts for the needs of AI data centers.\nThe turbine, whose breed is called Superpower, was first imagined as a component part of the Symphony engine that's supposed to power the Overture sometime in the future. And at the end of last month, it just got real.\nBoom said in late October that the first supersonic turbine core has been completed and is already on location at the company's test site at Colorado Air and Space Port in Watkins, Colorado. And it even has a name.\nGoing by Sprint Core, the prototype, which bundles together the compressor, combustor, and the high-pressure turbine itself, is not unlike the tech that'll go into the Superpower power generation solution and the Symphony supersonic engine.\nBoom will use this piece of hardware and the thousands of sensors that were strapped to it to calibrate the engineering tools at the Superfactory facility in Centennial, South Denver area, and optimize the production of the Superpower design.\nBy the looks of it, Boom has a better chance of succeeding in making the Overture supersonic plane compared to some of its competitors. The company says part of the money made with selling the Superpower solution (there is talk of an $1.25 billion backlog of orders for the tech) to artificial intelligence infrastructure providers will go into developing the aircraft.\nBoom said a while back the Overture had drawn the eye of operators the likes of United Airlines, American Airlines, and Japan Airlines, which have placed around 130 orders for the supersonic plane.\nIf and when the production of the airplane begins, Boom expects to roll out up to 66 planes per year at another Superfactory in Greensboro, North Carolina.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more articles from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nBoom Supersonic Overture superpower\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Daniel Patrascu\nDaniel loves writing (or so he claims), and he uses this skill to offer readers a \"behind the scenes\" look at the automotive industry. He also enjoys talking about space exploration and robots, because in his view the only way forward for humanity is away from this planet, in metal bodies. Full profile\nUP NEXT This Application Turns Your Outdated Tablet Into a New-Gen Android Auto-Like Head Unit",
